<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marino Sanudo Torsello</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1270–<a href="/years/1343">1343</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marino Sanudo Torsello was a Venetian statesman and geographer who travelled widely in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. His Secreta fidelium crucis proposed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> with an economic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and his history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> describes the Frankish states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, including the French-speaking court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marino-sanudo-torsello.jpg" alt="Marino Sanudo Torsello" width="320" height="292" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marino Sanuto the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bodleian_Library_MS._Tanner_190,_fol._4r,_initial.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sanudo was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> around 1270 into an aristocratic trading family active in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, a branch of which had founded the Duchy of the Archipelago on Naxos after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>; his father sat in the Venetian Senate. As a teenager he stayed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, the last stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a>, before its fall in 1291, and he later travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>. He is sometimes called Marino Sanuto the Elder, to tell him from the later Venetian diarist of the same name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He joined the entourage of Giovanni Dandolo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doge-of-venice">Doge of Venice</a>, was at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> in 1305 and then entered the service of Cardinal Riccardo Petroni at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, while also handling business for his family. His correspondents included the Venetian traveller Guglielmo Bernardi de Furvo, Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos">Andronikos II Palaiologos</a>, the Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. Why he took up the cause of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> is unclear: the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, or a patron who favoured a new crusade, may have moved him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His Secreta fidelium crucis, first written in 1306 and 1307 for Pope Clement V, stressed strategy and secure finance: a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> over several years, then the capture of the sultanate as a base for invading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, with manoeuvres planned and their daily costs budgeted. He kept expanding it with a history of the Holy Land and a geography of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, presented a new version to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a> in 1321 and sent a French translation to King Charles IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. At least eleven copies survive.</p><p>The treatise carries maps of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the earliest surviving medieval maps made for military strategy, and a world map joining the mappamundi with the accuracy of a portolan chart; the Genoese cartographer Pietro Vesconte was their main author. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> history of the Frankish principalities and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, written between 1326 and 1333, survives only in a Venetian translation, the Istoria del regno di <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, and gives a unique account of the recovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marino Sanuto the Elder” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Venetian writer <b>Marino Sanudo Torsello</b> reported that at the princes’ court in the Peloponnese French was spoken as well as in Paris.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1317" aria-label="Venice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4843 561 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Marino Sanudo Torsello"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5293 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5293 831h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1317">What was here in 1317? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
